      "content": "1. Distinguish between novel problem formulation (ORIG-PROB) and novel methodology (ORIG-MTH); a paper can score well on problem definition even if it lacks a new algorithm. 2. For ORIG-ANL, prioritize insights that challenge existing assumptions or reveal trade-offs (e.g., robustness conflicts) over standard performance benchmarks. 3. Critically evaluate ORIG-EXP by checking if datasets are too simple (e.g., MNIST) to support general claims; demand validation on complex benchmarks (e.g., CIFAR-10/ImageNet) for broad applicability. 4. Assess ORIG-COM by verifying if the combination of existing techniques is logically motivated and yields new capabilities, rather than being a superficial mix. 5. Recognize ORIG-NEG as a strength when the paper provides critical assessments or negative results that highlight limitations of popular methods (e.g., equivariance costs). 6. Ensure evidence anchors support claims about novelty by citing specific sections where the trade-off or insight is demonstrated.",
